# Distribution-bundle control (consumed by `git archive`).
#
# `git archive` produces a consumer bundle of the EAMOS machine — what you *operate* to prepare
# meetings, not this repository's own dev plumbing. The paths below are stripped via `export-ignore`:
#
#   * repo-management plumbing — CI / release / issue templates (.github/), .gitignore, this file,
#     and the guided installer (setup/, which *downloads* the bundle, so it ships as standalone
#     release assets, not inside the archive);
#   * dev-only docs — CONTRIBUTING.md (governance for work ON EAMOS), ROADMAP.md (the dev plan),
#     and SECURITY.md (how to report a vuln in the EAMOS project).
#
# What SHIPS in the bundle:
#   * .eamos-core/** — the machine (agent, orchestrator, tools, eval, docs incl. the RFCs/ADRs);
#   * the agent contract — AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md (auto-loaded from root) + README.md (entry point);
#   * LICENSE.
#
# Produce a bundle:  git archive --format=tar.gz -o pgs-eamos-bundle.tar.gz HEAD
#
# NOTE: patterns are anchored with a leading slash so they match only the intended path — the
# per-directory README.md files under .eamos-core/ are NOT stripped.

/.github                export-ignore
/.gitignore             export-ignore
/.gitattributes         export-ignore
/.idea                  export-ignore
/CONTRIBUTING.md        export-ignore
/ROADMAP.md             export-ignore
/SECURITY.md            export-ignore
# The guided installer downloads the bundle, so it is not part of it: published as standalone
# release assets (setup.{sh,command,ps1,bat} + SHA256SUMS), not shipped inside the archive.
/setup                  export-ignore

# Installer line endings, pinned regardless of the committer's platform: LF for the POSIX shebang
# scripts and for setup.ps1 (pwsh reads LF fine on every OS); CRLF for the cmd .bat shim.
setup/*.sh              text eol=lf
setup/*.command         text eol=lf
setup/*.ps1             text eol=lf
setup/*.bat             text eol=crlf
